    {
      "dom_id": "question-26721",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14178562/assets/2026a71a7cb647e7a5d844e9c5259f73321d7fd8097255245dc99f87dfc60a92.png)\n\nThe straight line shown above intersects the $y$-axis when $y=5$ and intersects the $x$-axis when $x=7.$ Find its slope.",
      "worked_solution": "Note that the line passes through the points $(0,5)$ and $(7,0).$ So, we let $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(0,5)$ and $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(7,0),$ and compute the slope using the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0-5}{7-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{-5}{7} \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the line is $-\\frac{5}{7}.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
